{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## The defect\n\nThe close precheck requires EVERY `- [ ]` acceptance criterion to be ticked before a ticket can\nclose. It has no way to record that a criterion is NOT APPLICABLE.\n\nThis breaks on any ticket whose ACs are branch-exclusive — the common \"decide X, then: if A do\nthis / if B do that\" shape, where satisfying one branch necessarily means the other is never\nperformed.\n\n## Confirmed instance 2026-08-05\n\nBug `a573-00ea-2bf6-4eb1` asked for an operator decision (honor the inert\n`CodeHealthConfig.enabled` field, or remove it) and carried both outcomes as ACs:\n\n    - [ ] If HONORED: structural metric evaluation is gated on it, with a test proving ...\n    - [ ] If REMOVED: the field, its coercion row and its config-surface entries go ...\n\nThe operator chose REMOVE. The implementing agent therefore could not satisfy the HONORED\nbranch — by construction, not by omission. The close gate refused the close until that box was\nticked, so the agent ticked a criterion describing work it had deliberately not done, and\nposted a comment stating the tick means N/A rather than done. It did NOT use `--force-close`,\nwhich was the right call.\n\nTicket authorship is a contributing cause and worth saying plainly: whoever writes a\ndecide-then-branch ticket creates this trap. But the gate offering no expression for N/A is\nwhat converts an awkward ticket into a false record.\n\n## Why this is worth fixing rather than tolerating\n\nA ticked AC is load-bearing evidence in this system. The close gate reads it, the completion\nverifier reasons over it, and reviewers treat it as an assertion that the work was done. Once\nagents learn that ticking can mean \"not applicable\", the signal degrades for every ticket, not\njust branch-exclusive ones — and the degradation is invisible, because a ticked box looks\nidentical either way.\n\nThe `[operator-attested]` tag already establishes the precedent that a criterion can be\nsatisfied by something other than in-repo work. This is the same shape: a criterion that is\nlegitimately not code-verifiable, for a different reason.\n\n## Candidate approaches (weigh; not prescribed)\n\n1. An explicit marker, e.g. `- [x] [n/a: <reason>]` or `- [~]`, that the precheck accepts and\n   that records WHY. Mirrors `[operator-attested]`, so the vocabulary stays consistent.\n2. Structured branch groups, so ACs can be declared mutually exclusive and the gate requires\n   exactly one branch satisfied.\n3. Author-side only: forbid branch-exclusive ACs and require the decision to be made before the\n   ACs are written. Cheapest, but relies on discipline and does not help tickets already filed.\n\nOption 3 alone is insufficient — the decide-then-branch shape is genuinely useful when a ticket\nis filed before the operator has ruled, which is exactly how a573 and dce2 were written.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria (rescoped 2026-08-07 to the refined ruling)\n\nN/A eligibility keys off the CLOSE DISPOSITION, not off the individual criterion. A ticket claiming\nCOMPLETION keeps today's strict behaviour; a ticket closing as a terminal disposition (won't-fix,\nobsolete, not-a-bug, duplicate) has no applicable AC scan.\n\n- [ ] The set of close classes that make the AC scan INAPPLICABLE is decided and named explicitly.\n      `--class` already exists on bug closes (regression, plan_defect, env_integration, flaky,\n      preexisting, not_a_bug, duplicate, escalated, undetermined); state which of these are terminal\n      dispositions, and whether a won't-fix / obsolete class needs adding — 3b92 had no honest class\n      to use and was force-closed as a result\n- [ ] Closing under a terminal-disposition class does NOT run the unchecked-AC precheck, and records\n      the class as the reason the criteria were not evaluated\n- [ ] Closing as COMPLETED is UNCHANGED: an unmet criterion still fails the close, and the remedy is\n      still to remediate the criterion. A test pins that this path did not weaken\n- [ ] A test proves a terminal-disposition close succeeds with unchecked criteria present AND that the\n      same ticket closing as completed still fails\n- [ ] `--force-close` is no longer the routine answer for an obsolete ticket; the CLI message on a\n      failed close points at the class mechanism rather than straight at the override (this is the\n      steering defect 218e reports separately)\n- [ ] Non-bug types (task, story, epic) are covered too, or their exclusion is stated with a reason —\n      3b92, 5dd2, e5ec, 39c5 and 83f2 were all tasks, and all were force-closed\n- [ ] The distinction is documented where AC authors will see it, alongside `[operator-attested]`\n\n## Out of scope\n\nA PER-CRITERION N/A marker. That was the original request and it is rejected: on a ticket claiming\ncompletion, a criterion nobody can satisfy means the plan was written wrong, and letting an author\nmark it N/A converts that signal into a checkbox. The disposition-level mechanism above solves the\ngenuine case without opening that door."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786097741199449001,"uuid":"67b5d42b-ca3b-4dbc-8635-41dcf1fc55f7"}